      "index": 0,
      "text": "<think>\nTo find the product of 375 and 775, I'll start by breaking down the multiplication into more manageable parts.\n\nFirst, I'll multiply 375 by 700. This is straightforward because multiplying by 700 is the same as multiplying by 7 and then by 100. So, 375 times 7 equals 2625, and multiplying by 100 gives me 262,500.\n\nNext, I'll multiply 375 by 75. To make this easier, I'll break 75 down into 70 and 5. Multiplying 375 by 70 gives me 26,250, and multiplying 375 by 5 gives me 1,875. Adding these two results together, 26,250 plus 1,875 equals 28,125.\n\nFinally, I'll add the two main parts together: 262,500 plus 28,125 equals 290,625. This gives me the final product of 375 and 775.\n</think>\n\n# Solution\n\nTo find the product of **375** and **775**, we'll follow these steps:\n\n1. **Break down the multiplication:**\n   \n   \\[\n   375 \\times 775 = 375 \\times (700 + 75)\n   \\]\n\n2. **Multiply 375 by 700:**\n   \n   \\[\n   375 \\times 700 = 375 \\times 7 \\times 100 = 2625 \\times 100 = 262,\\!500\n   \\]\n\n3. **Multiply 375 by 75:**\n   \n   \\[\n   375 \\times 75 = 375 \\times (70 + 5) = (375 \\times 70) + (375 \\times 5)\n   \\]\n   \n   - **Calculate \\(375 \\times 70\\):**\n     \n     \\[\n     375 \\times 70 = 375 \\times 7 \\times 10 = 2625 \\times 10 = 26,\\!250\n     \\]\n   \n   - **Calculate \\(375 \\times 5\\):**\n     \n     \\[\n     375 \\times 5 = 1,\\!875\n     \\]\n   \n   - **Add the two results:**\n     \n     \\[\n     26,\\!250 + 1,\\!875 = 28,\\!125\n     \\]\n\n4. **Add the results from steps 2 and 3:**\n   \n   \\[\n   262,\\!500 + 28,\\!125 = 290,\\!625\n   \\]\n\n**Final Answer:**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{290,\\!625}\n\\]",
